Coastal wetlands, including salt marshes, mangroves, and seagrass beds, act as nature’s first line of defense against storm surges and coastal flooding. Their ability to mitigate damage is not merely theoretical; it is a physical reality driven by fluid dynamics and ecological structure.

The primary mechanism at work is energy dissipation. When a storm pushes water toward the shore, the wetland vegetation—whether tall grasses, shrubs, or trees—creates significant friction against the moving water. As waves and surge flow through this dense root systems and stems, kinetic energy is converted into turbulence and heat. This process